WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
Actual local app with saved research data
Edited and narrated still states

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.605
BioGraph Steward builds on Pharos with added source context,
traceable

00:00:08.605 --> 00:00:14.117
answers and recoverable graph qualification. In our ninety-nine-
target workspace, we

00:00:14.117 --> 00:00:21.186
add eighty-seven sequence-qualified physical-association pairs and
sixty-eight protein-location links absent

00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:25.531
from the captured Pharos annotations. The agent makes this context

00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:28.580
searchable and traceable. Here is one example.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.750
One concrete addition: KRAS–CDC42BPB

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.032
Here is one concrete addition. For KRAS and CDC42BPB, the

00:00:36.032 --> 00:00:40.845
source passport shows a qualified physical association absent from
our

00:00:40.845 --> 00:00:45.658
captured Pharos graph. The same record preserves STRING release
twelve

00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:49.183
point five, its score of two hundred and three, and

00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:54.064
the sequence identity checks. The agent actually selected and
inspected

00:00:54.064 --> 00:00:58.877
this citation, and proposed keeping it available as candidate
context.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.050
Matched source-view recovery
Full table also retains coarse-map Pharos control: 44/51

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.509
Published protein-location labels give us a measurable comparison.
Using the

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:13.413
same ontology-qualified mapping, captured Pharos recovers forty-
one of fifty-one labels;

00:01:13.413 --> 00:01:19.318
qualified native annotations recover forty-four. In the already-
used OpenCell reference,

00:01:19.318 --> 00:01:23.630
recovery rises from thirty of forty to thirty-one. The qualified

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.810
native source recovers additional recorded biological context.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.050
The agent turns a question into inspectable evidence

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.779
The NVIDIA Nemotron agent makes that evidence easier to
investigate.

00:01:35.779 --> 00:01:39.841
It plans a supported source query, the application executes the

00:01:39.841 --> 00:01:44.537
validated tool, and the agent prioritizes citations for
inspection. Every

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:48.408
original record under a selected citation is opened before a

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:52.660
grounded review proposal is produced. In our fixed comparison, all

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.674
five allowed queries preserved the deterministic tools' complete
results. This

00:01:57.674 --> 00:02:02.053
saved example displays eight of fourteen matching proteins under
its

00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:03.068
recorded limit.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.250
Explore the graph; save your own review
Public demo is read-only; editable local copy shown

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.832
To explore an answer, expand a result to read its

00:02:09.832 --> 00:02:13.516
summary, then choose Show in graph to focus the diagram.

00:02:13.516 --> 00:02:17.977
The shared demo is read-only. In an editing-enabled local
workspace,

00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:22.307
select Record evidence review. Enter a reviewer, choose a decision

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:25.281
and add your reason. Save review in a new run

00:02:25.281 --> 00:02:29.870
creates a reviewed copy and preserves the original. This
demonstration

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.555
saves a provisional decision, not biological acceptance.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.250
Historical partially known biological-process study
Rendered saved report; not an agent prediction

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.376
We also reused the published ProtBoost approach in a separate

00:02:45.376 --> 00:02:51.100
protein-function study. The primary task predicts additional
biological-process functions for

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.120
proteins with some functions already known. On a historical graph

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.566
of twenty thousand four hundred and twelve proteins, fixed-
ontology Fmax

00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:02.550
rose from about point one seven two to point two

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.691
one zero. The paired ninety-five percent gain interval stayed
above

00:03:06.691 --> 00:03:10.832
zero in both reference views. Annotation updates alone also
helped.

00:03:10.832 --> 00:03:14.791
This study is separate from the agent and the ninety-nine-target

00:03:14.791 --> 00:03:15.095
app.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.550
Qualification with recoverable decisions

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:25.671
The retained graph example adds another improvement: explicit
identifier qualification.

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:30.195
It excludes two hundred and ninety-eight records using obsolete
Gene

00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:35.704
Ontology identifiers, while retaining sixteen thousand six hundred
and eighty-seven

00:03:35.704 --> 00:03:40.360
records. The original sources, input graph and decision reasons
remain

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.557
recoverable, so a colleague can inspect or revisit each change.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.750
How to interpret these measured gains

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.945
These are qualified annotation-recovery results, not whole-graph
precision. Shared sources

00:03:53.945 --> 00:03:58.955
and prior reference use limit independence. Physical association
is candidate

00:03:58.955 --> 00:04:04.094
context, not confirmed direct binding. Additional primary learning
benefit over

00:04:04.094 --> 00:04:07.113
fully refreshed annotations remains uncertain.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.350
A more useful, inspectable starting point

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.340
The result is a more useful starting point for research:

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:20.024
additional qualified context, measurable source coverage, a
source-inspection agent, and

00:04:20.024 --> 00:04:24.814
recoverable graph decisions. Start with a biological question,
inspect the

00:04:24.814 --> 00:04:28.710
evidence behind the answer, and share the complete record so

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.073
the next researcher can build on it.
